<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  ket.app's own executable. Under the hardened runtime macOS refuses the
  microphone, the camera and Apple Events to a process whose signature does
  not claim them, without asking the person first, so each one here is
  something ket would otherwise silently lose:

  - audio-input: dictation in the quick prompt (crates/ket-ui/src/voice.rs),
    and a web page in a browser tab asking for the microphone.
  - camera: a web page asking for the camera, which is why Info.plist carries
    NSCameraUsageDescription (scripts/chromium-dev.sh).
  - automation.apple-events: the programs ket's terminals run. macOS holds
    ket responsible for them, so `osascript` from a shell in ket asks under
    ket's name, and is refused outright without this.

  No code-signing exceptions (allow-jit and the like): the browser process
  compiles no JavaScript, which is why Chromium's own app-entitlements.plist
  has none either; the renderer and GPU helpers carry their own. Library
  validation stays on, because a Developer ID signature gives the app, the CEF
  framework and every helper the same Team ID.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
